      "id": "cor-unbracketed-and-unordered-tensor-strings-are-well-defined-in-a-symmetric-monoidal-category",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "overstrong-title-or-statement",
      "evidence": "The Statement says that any two parenthesised reorderings of a finite list are \"canonically and uniquely identified\" without fixing occurrence labels or an induced permutation. For a repeated factor X, the unlabelled source and target can both be X\\otimes X, yet there are two canonical endomorphisms: 1 and c_{X,X}. In the scoped example ex-the-sign-braiding-on-supervector-spaces, for a purely odd one-dimensional X, its displayed formula gives c_{X,X}(v\\otimes v)=-v\\otimes v, so c_{X,X}\\ne1 over a field of characteristic not 2. The cited symmetric-coherence theorem only gives equality for composites with the same underlying permutation; it does not make 1 and the transposition equal after repeated occurrences are forgotten.",
      "severity": "fatal",
